          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong> Predicate logic
            , also called
            %strong first-order logic
            and sometimes abbreviated
            = succeed "," do
              %strong FOL
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            used in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
          %p
            In contrast to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/0th-order+logic"} 0th-order logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+variable"} bound
            by
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantification"} quantifiers
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"}> for all
            ”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall
            and “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/existential+quantifier"} there exists
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists
          %p
            (This means that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of 1st order logic is given by
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyperdoctrines"} hyperdoctrines
          %p
            However, in contrast to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %p
            A
            %strong predicate calculus
            is simply a system for describing and working with predicate logic. The precise form of such a calculus (and hence of the logic itself) depends on whether one is using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} Typed
            predicate logic may be called
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory#Logic+over+type+theory"} first-order logic over type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            is often identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            is often identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+category"} Boolean category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+type+theory"} simply typed
            predicate logic with only one type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+of+discourse"} domain of discourse
            or the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong universe of discourse
          %p
            For many (perhaps most?) authors, predicate logic is really
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                predicate logic with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} =(x,y)
            (also written
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x=y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x = y \wedge \phi(x))\implies \phi(y)
            for any predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            with one argument. However, some forms of predicate logic do not include an equality primitive, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FOLDS"} FOLDS
            (in whose name ‘FOL’ stands for ‘first-order logic’). In some first-order theories, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
          %p
            In constructive mathematics, one could also have a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                predicate logic with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness"} apartness
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo #
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \#(x,y)
            (also written
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo #
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\#y
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+relation"} apartness relation
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+equality"} propositional equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi y
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo #
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x = y \coloneqq \neg(x \# y)
            satisfying the rule of substitution:
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ¬
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo #
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\neg(x \# y) \wedge \phi(x))\implies \phi(y)
            for any predicate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            with one argument. This makes the domain of discourse into an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/apartness+space"} apartness space
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lindstr%C3%B6m%27s+theorems"} Lindström's theorems
            give important abstract characterizations of classical untyped first-order logic.
